Branches foster inter-national relations in their Pro-grammes:— “Anglo - American Relations” and “American Life” (Thames Valley, Torquay, Hud-dersfield, Coventry, Nottingham); “History, People and Customs of Poland” (Sunderland); “Housing in Newfoundland” (Warrington); “British West Indies ” (New-castle); “Russia” (Birmingham, Sevenoaks, Edinburgh); “Chang-ing China” and films of India and America (Coventry); “Canada” and “South Africa” (Hudders-field); “Norway” and “Ceylon” (Aylesbury); “Evacuation from France” (Bristol); “Social Ser-vices and Health” (Plymouth). Current Affairs
Current affairs are indicated by “Life and Work in the A.T.S.” (Liverpool); “Mastery of the Mediterranean ” (Sunderland) ;
“The Merchant Navy” (Birming-ham); “The Beveridge Report” (Sheffield); “Dig for Victory” (Liverpool, Torquay); “First Aid in the Home” (Liverpool, Stoke, Edinburgh); and in the electrical sense by talks on present fuel economies and post-war electrical development at Warrington, Mid-Gloucester, Burnley, Watford, Manchester, Edinburgh, Falkirk, Bradford, Aylesbury, Sheffield, Skipton, Rugby, Leicester, Tun-bridge Wells, Wallasey. Psycho-logy and psychiatric social work have interested Burnley and Sunderland; Aylesbury enjoyed “A Day in the Life of a Nurse”; Manchester a talk on “The Uses of Plastics”; Walsall heard of “Beauty Spots and History of Walsall” and about “Women and Local Government” from their woman Mayor.

